  <h1>What is a webhook?</h1>
  <div class="artmeta"><span>By Coldwast</span><span>Updated Nov 19, 2026</span><span>9 min read</span><span class="tag">#webhook</span><span class="tag">#api</span><span class="tag">#web</span></div>

  <figure>
    <img class="hero" src={hero} alt="A server and a network switch connected by an orange Ethernet cable" width="1200" height="420" loading="eager" />
    <figcaption>A server wired into a network switch - a webhook is an automated message one server sends to another the moment an event happens.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>You set up a payment, and your app reacts the instant the money clears. You push a commit, and a build starts on its own. Behind that immediacy is almost always a <strong>webhook</strong>. This guide explains, plainly, what a webhook is, how it differs from a normal API call, how to receive one safely, and where you have already been relying on them.</p>

  <p class="note">To receive webhooks you need an always-on server with a public HTTPS URL, which a laptop that sleeps is not. A <a href="/go/cloud.html" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank">DigitalOcean droplet</a> or a <a href="https://www.tkqlhce.com/click-101807052-17389597?sid=coldwa-en-webhook-top" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Contabo Cloud VPS</a> (affiliate links) can run the Python receiver below (standard library only, port 8080) behind a <a href="/e/blog/2026-06-26-what-is-a-reverse-proxy.html">reverse proxy</a> that serves HTTPS.</p>

  <h2>The short definition</h2>
  <p>A <strong>webhook is an automated HTTP request a service sends to a URL you control when an event happens.</strong> Rather than you asking a service for updates, the service notifies <em>you</em> the moment there is news.</p>
  <p>That is why webhooks are often called a <strong>&quot;reverse API&quot;</strong>. With a normal <a href="/e/blog/2026-06-14-what-is-an-api.html">API</a> you are the client: you make the request and the server answers. With a webhook the roles flip - the provider becomes the client and <em>your</em> endpoint is the server it calls.</p>

  <h2>Webhook vs polling an API</h2>
  <p>Before webhooks, the usual way to stay up to date was <strong>polling</strong>: call an API on a timer (&quot;anything new yet?&quot;) over and over. Polling is simple but wasteful - most calls return nothing, and there is always a lag between the event and your next check.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Polling</strong> - you ask repeatedly. Many wasted requests, delayed reaction, simple to build.</li>
    <li><strong>Webhook</strong> - the service tells you once, immediately. Efficient and near real-time, but you must run an endpoint to receive it.</li>
  </ul>

  <figure><img src="/img/what-is-a-webhook-2026-servers.jpg" alt="Three black server towers with glowing green upward arrow indicators" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="700" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" /><figcaption>Servers pushing data outward - a webhook fires when one service POSTs an event to another service&#39;s endpoint.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>How a webhook works, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>You register a <strong>URL</strong> (your endpoint) with the provider and choose which events you care about.</li>
    <li>When such an event happens, the provider sends an HTTP <strong>POST</strong> to your URL with a body (usually JSON) describing it.</li>
    <li>Your server reads the payload, returns a quick <strong>2xx</strong> status to acknowledge it, and does the real work afterwards.</li>
    <li>If your endpoint is down or returns an error, many providers (Stripe, for example) <strong>retry</strong> with a growing delay - which is why duplicates can happen. GitHub does not retry on its own (see below).</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A delivery looks much like any HTTP request:</p>
  <pre><code>POST /webhooks/payments  HTTP/1.1
Host: yourapp.com
X-Signature: t=1718600000,v1=9f86d08...

&#123; &quot;event&quot;: &quot;payment.succeeded&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pay_42&quot;, &quot;amount&quot;: 1999 &#125;</code></pre>

  <h2>Real examples you have already met</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Payments</strong> - Stripe and PayPal send a webhook when a charge succeeds, fails or is refunded, so your app updates the order without polling.</li>
    <li><strong>Code hosting</strong> - GitHub and GitLab fire a webhook on push, pull request or issue, which is how CI builds and bots are triggered.</li>
    <li><strong>Messaging</strong> - Slack, Discord and chat platforms use incoming webhooks to post messages and to notify your app of events.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Receiving one safely</h2>
  <p>A robust receiver does four things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Serve HTTPS</strong> and accept <strong>POST</strong>; reply with a fast 2xx, then process asynchronously.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify the signature</strong> - providers sign the body (an HMAC with a shared secret). Reject anything that fails.</li>
    <li><strong>Be idempotent</strong> - the same event may arrive more than once; use the event id so re-delivery is harmless.</li>
    <li><strong>Return errors honestly</strong> - if you cannot process it, return a non-2xx so the provider retries instead of dropping it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Webhooks need an <strong>always-on endpoint</strong> with a public HTTPS URL - which is why a small server or cloud instance is the usual home for a webhook receiver, rather than a machine that sleeps.</p>


  <h2>Your first webhook receiver, tested</h2>
  <p>This Python receiver uses only the standard library. It checks the HMAC SHA-256 signature in the <code>X-Hub-Signature-256</code> header (the <code>sha256=...</code> format GitHub uses), ignores an event it has already seen by its <code>id</code>, and answers <code>204</code> as soon as it has recorded it. We ran it on Nov 19, 2026 with Python 3.12.3.</p>
  <pre><code>import hmac, hashlib, json
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer

SECRET = b"change-this-secret"
seen = set()

class Receiver(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    def do_POST(self):
        body = self.rfile.read(int(self.headers.get("Content-Length", 0)))
        expected = "sha256=" + hmac.new(SECRET, body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
        if not hmac.compare_digest(expected, self.headers.get("X-Hub-Signature-256", "")):
            self.send_response(401); self.end_headers(); return
        event = json.loads(body)
        if event["id"] in seen:
            print("duplicate ignored:", event["id"])
        else:
            seen.add(event["id"])
            print("event received:", event["event"], event["id"])
        self.send_response(204); self.end_headers()

HTTPServer(("0.0.0.0", 8080), Receiver).serve_forever()</code></pre>
  <p>To play the sender from another terminal, we sign the body with the same secret and send it twice (a retry), then once with a fake signature:</p>
  <pre><code>BODY='&#123;"event":"payment.succeeded","id":"pay_42"&#125;'
SIG="sha256=$(printf '%s' "$BODY" | openssl dgst -sha256 -hmac 'change-this-secret' | sed 's/^.*= //')"
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%&#123;http_code&#125;\n' -X POST localhost:8080 -H "X-Hub-Signature-256: $SIG" -d "$BODY"
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%&#123;http_code&#125;\n' -X POST localhost:8080 -H "X-Hub-Signature-256: $SIG" -d "$BODY"
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%&#123;http_code&#125;\n' -X POST localhost:8080 -H "X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=fake" -d "$BODY"</code></pre>
  <p>Real output: curl prints <code>204</code>, <code>204</code> and <code>401</code>. The receiver prints:</p>
  <pre><code>event received: payment.succeeded pay_42
duplicate ignored: pay_42</code></pre>
  <p>The first two requests get <code>204</code>: the sender knows not to retry, and the duplicate is not processed twice. The fake signature gets <code>401</code> and touches nothing. For production, store the <code>id</code> values in a database instead of memory, and put the receiver behind HTTPS (for example with a <a href="/e/blog/2026-06-26-what-is-a-reverse-proxy.html">reverse proxy</a> such as Caddy or Nginx).</p>

  <h2>If your server does not answer: GitHub vs Stripe</h2>
  <p>Senders do not treat a failed delivery the same way, and that decides where to host your receiver:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>GitHub</strong>: if your server is down or takes longer than 10 seconds, the delivery is recorded as failed and <strong>is not retried automatically</strong>; you redeliver it from the webhook settings or through the REST API (<a href="https://docs.github.com/webhooks/using-webhooks/handling-failed-webhook-deliveries" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GitHub docs</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Stripe</strong>: in live mode it resends automatically for <strong>up to three days</strong> with a growing delay (<a href="https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks/process-undelivered-events" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Stripe docs</a>).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Either way, a laptop that goes to sleep or a free tier that spins down after a few idle minutes loses events. A webhook receiver lives best on a small, always-on VPS.</p>

